+For retryable authenticated delivery, pass a stable `inputId`:
+
+```ts
+const source = from("conversation-42");
+const session = await source.send("Hello", { auth, inputId: "message-17" });
+const receipt = session.acceptedInput;
+const recovered = await source.getInputAcceptance("message-17", auth);
+```
+
+The receipt identifies the durable native input event and canonical session. Read-only lookup
+returns `undefined` if that principal and destination have no accepted input with that ID. Use
+`Session.getInputAcceptance(inputId, auth)` to recover inputs sent through a fixed session handle;
+fixed-session and continuation namespaces are distinct. `ChannelReceiveContext.attachSession(id)`
+binds an I/O-free native handle to that exact ID and carries channel delivery metadata and default
+turn policy. It does not resolve the current address owner, send input, or prove acceptance; use
+it inside `receive(input, ctx)` as `return ctx.attachSession(receipt.sessionId)` after establishing
+the canonical receipt and current authority. Lookup does not validate a new payload
+or wake work; retry the original send to do both. Changed content under the same ID conflicts.
+Receipts survive hook disposal while native events remain retained. Stable payloads must be
+finite, acyclic JSON; use URL file parts instead of binary buffers. Acceptance does not imply
+processing completion or external delivery.
+
+A committed input event and its workflow wake are separate native writes. A process can die
+between them. `getInputAcceptance` remains strictly read-only and cannot repair that gap.
+After checking the stored input, current authority and transport lease, call
+`source.recoverInputAcceptance(inputId, auth)` and require the same receipt before marking the
+transport accepted. This operation reads the actual native receipt again and republishes the
+canonical nonterminal run's wake; it needs neither content nor artifacts and never resends input.
+Use `session.recoverInputAcceptance(inputId, auth)` for the fixed-session namespace. Repeated
+recovery can publish another wake, but native event replay consumes the existing input once.
+Terminal runs return the same receipt without queue publication. Missing receipts return
+`undefined`; queue/storage errors remain uncertain and must not be treated as acceptance.
+Recovery does not grant application authority, validate a replacement payload, or prove completion.
+
+Retain native events for the entire input recovery lifetime. This API has no retention watermark
+or expired-receipt result: after event purging or a restore that loses an accepted event,
+`undefined` cannot distinguish that lost receipt from an input never accepted. Block automatic
+retries when retention is no longer guaranteed. A rejected lookup or timeout is unknown, never
+absence. The PostgreSQL World has no automatic event TTL; hook retention settings do not govern
+input receipt retention.
+
+Persist the original authenticated principal and exact destination before sending. Address
+rekeying does not migrate receipts; changing a principal, its issuer/authenticator, or a target
+creates a different lookup scope. Query the original scope after a reset or move, while separately
+rechecking current authority before any retry. Lookup itself does not check application grants.
+
+Keyed continuation delivery includes the effective mode, input capability, channel state,
+initiator auth and title in its content comparison. Reuse the original options when retrying.
+The native session owner binds these options for its initial key before acceptance; a concurrent
+initial request with different configuration conflicts. New input keys sent to an existing
+continuation use the existing session configuration, as other continuation deliveries do.
